> If I have a matrix TxN, with missings, how do I take the last value from each column that isn't missing, in Julia?
**assistant**:
> To extract the last non-missing value from each column of a matrix with missing values in Julia, you can use the `skipmissing` function in combination with a loop or the `mapslices` function. Below is one way to achieve this using a comprehension:
> 
> ### Using a Loop
> 
> ```julia
> using Missing
> 
> # Assuming `matrix` is your input matrix with missings
> # Create a matrix with some missing values for demonstration
> matrix = [1.0 2.0 missing; 
>           3.0 missing 6.0; 
>           missing 5.0 7.0; 
>           8.0 9.0 missing]
> 
> # Preallocate a vector to store the last non-missing values
> last_non_missing = Vector{Union{Float64, Missing}}(missing, size(matrix, 2))
> 
> # Loop over each column
> for j in 1:size(matrix, 2)
>     # Get the current column and skip missing values
>     col_values = matrix[:, j]
>     non_missing_values = skipmissing(col_values)
>     
>     # Store the last value if it exists
>     last_non_missing[j] = isempty(non_missing_values) ? missing : last(non_missing_values)
> end
> 
> println(last_non_missing)
> ```
> 
> ### Using `mapslices`
> 
> Alternatively, you can use `mapslices` to apply a function to each column:
> 
> ```julia
> using Missing
> 
> matrix = [1.0 2.0 missing; 
>           3.0 missing 6.0; 
>           missing 5.0 7.0; 
>           8.0 9.0 missing]
> 
> # Use mapslices with a lambda function to find the last non-missing value in each column
> last_non_missing = mapslices(x -> isempty(skipmissing(x)) ? missing : last(skipmissing(x)), matrix, dims=1)
> 
> println(last_non_missing)
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation
> - **`skipmissing(col_values)`**: This function creates an iterable of the non-missing values from the column.
> - **`last(non_missing_values)`**: This retrieves the last element from the iterable of non-missing values.
> - The conditional check for `isempty(non_missing_values)` ensures that we handle cases where there might be no non-missing values gracefully.
> 
> You can use either method based on your preference. Let me know if you need further assistance!
